# This repo is checked out on both Windows (core.autocrlf=true) and accessed
# from WSL/Linux (via the same checkout, over /mnt/c) as one shared working
# copy - not two separate clones, one working tree. Windows' git and WSL's
# git each have their own core.autocrlf default (true vs unset), so without
# this file the two disagree about what a "clean" checkout of the same file
# looks like:
#   - bash/* actually broke functionally: Windows checked them out with
#     CRLF, and bash fails to source a script with \r in it at all (a stray
#     \r breaks `foo() {` one-liners and corrupts `source ~/.bash_git` into
#     a path that doesn't exist) - looked like "nothing got set up" even
#     though the symlinks themselves were fine.
#   - Everything else (*.ps1/*.json/*.lua) was only cosmetic - CRLF works
#     fine there - but WSL's git, with no autocrlf conversion of its own,
#     compared the CRLF working tree against the LF blob Windows' git
#     stored and reported them as permanently "modified" with no real
#     content change, purely from the two git installs disagreeing.
# Force LF everywhere so both sides agree regardless of local autocrlf.
* text=auto eol=lf
